Unknown option: --no-such-option

Usage:
    clean-folder-structure [options] <inputDir>
Options:
    -y, --sort-into-years
                    Sort files whose names start with a date, in either of the forms the
                    names carry - "YYYYMMDD Filename.ext" or "YYYY-MM-DD
                    Filename.ext" - into yearly subfolders "YYYY/"; the file
                    keeps the form its name has.
    -d, --fix-dates Before cleaning file names, normalise a leading date of the
                    form YYYY<sep>MM<sep>DD<sep> (separators: space . / _ -) at
                    the start of a file name into the form the dateForm setting
                    names - the compact "YYYYMMDD " form, or "YYYY-MM-DD " with
                    the setting set to iso - keeping the rest of the name.
                    Applied across all subfolders.
    -n, --number-files
                    Clean folder names as usual, but instead of cleaning file
                    names, just number the files in each folder (01, 02, ...
                    naturally sorted and zero-padded). Only the plurality
                    (most common) filetype in a folder is numbered; other
                    files (e.g. a cover.jpg) are left alone.
    -f, --fragments <file>
                    Read the name fragments to remove from <file> (one per line,
                    '#' comments and blank lines ignored) instead of from
                    data/fragments.txt in the medialib data directory. Without
                    this option that file is used when it is there, and names
                    are cleaned without any fragment removal when it is not.
    -p, --preview   Preview: do not touch the input at all. Mirror its structure
                    into a sandbox, run the full cleaning there, and write
                    "before.tree" and "after.tree" into the input folder so the
                    result can be reviewed and tested risk-free. Combines with the
                    other options (-y, -d, -n) to preview their effect too.
    -h, --help      Print this help page.
    -A, --ai        Let language models help (docs/models.md). Without -A no
                    step asks one; the commands this one starts are handed it.
    -v, --verbose   Also say why each item was handled the way it was, and on a
                    failure print the tool's whole output rather than its last
                    lines.
    -q, --quiet     Print errors only - no progress and no warnings.
    -x, --set <name=value>
                    Override a setting for this run, over the settings file;
                    repeatable. A secret is refused - the settings file is where
                    it goes. The settings page lists the names.

